{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfyEtR67eWaHKNj87DbRp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Qh7e12TgVJBTYQKyodD7H3","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q019_stable_periodic_release_semantics","answer":"The current-release endpoint (/api/v1/releases/current, fetched 2026-10-12) reports SCOPe 2.08 as the current public release. Its stable label is \"2.08-stable\"; it was frozen on 2021-07-28 and released on 2021-09-22. The record has is_public = true and is_comprehensive = false. The latest periodic update listed is \"2.08-2026-08-16\", dated 2026-08-16.\n\nFor a reproducible analysis, cite the stable release, SCOPe 2.08 (2.08-stable), and pin release=2.08 in every API call, for example /api/v1/releases/2.08/.... Don't rely on a moving \"current\" or \"latest\" alias. If your results depend on entries added after the 2.08 freeze, also record the exact periodic identifier (2.08-2026-08-16) and the date you retrieved the data.\n\nThe periodic label marks a dated update built on top of the 2.08 stable base. It adds newer PDB entries and chains to the classification, mostly through automated methods. It is not a new curated stable release: the identifier keeps the 2.08 prefix and adds a date, and later updates will replace it. So the stable label refers to a fixed, citable snapshot. The periodic label refers to a changing update stream, and data from it is only reproducible if you give the exact dated version.\n\nLimits: the endpoint only provides these labels and dates. It does not say how many entries the periodic update added or which assignments were curated rather than automated. My description of what periodic updates contain comes from general knowledge of SCOPe, not from this endpoint. Release metadata on its own says nothing about family, superfamily or fold classification, or about homology.","evidence_used":["mcp__scope__get_scop_service_info","/api/v1/","